true to Fumito away does work on eco and  Shadow of the Colossus The Last Guardian  is a fascinating game that portrays a  heartfelt relationship between two  unlikely cohorts a nameless boy and a  giant creature named Treecko who  developed mutual trust communication and  compassion against seemingly impossible  odds their tale is a shining example of  storytelling through subtle cues and  shared experiences the occasional  annoyances that come from trying to  force two members of different species  to cooperate is part and parcel of their  realistic partnership for better and for  worse what an unshakable final act melts  these grievances away leaving you  emotionally invested in the outcome of  Treecko and the boys incredible journey  Oh does I'm a Kakuna RIA Tania it begins  when we find them mysteriously  imprisoned in a dank cave though the  cause of their plight remains a mystery  until the end the need to escape is  immediately understood the environment  rather than guards stand in your way at  the start you only have direct control  over the boy and while you are free to  move around Treecko is subdued by a  metal collar fractured armor and a pair  of spheres lodged in his back your  continued efforts to relieve his burden  slowly but surely earned treecko's  favour in short order he's free from his  shackles and the two of you begin your  tricky escape Treecko and you are  instrumental to each other's progress  it's easy for you to slip through small  passageways and precisely manipulate  objects to activate doors but only  Treecko can leap dozens of feet into the  air and reach high out-of-the-way places  you need to rely on physical positioning  and vocal commands to direct him and  there are moments that could be  considered traditional combat but you  strive to escape  allowing treecko's animal instincts to  kick in as he willingly crushes enemies  under his feet  success in the Last Guardian typically  comes down to identifying the one object  or passage in an environment that allows  you to move ahead the process of  elimination as you peek around corners  and climb piles of rubble in search of  your next step can be taxing as you move  with palpable inertia knocking into  walls and sliding across dusty stone  floors in the process both products of  convincing animation that other games  rarely employ whether by design or  circumstance the duas initially troubled  communication manifests itself  throughout the first half of your  journey when treecko's involved in  troubleshooting puzzles you must endure  his slow reaction times and decipher his  body language during your investigation  a process that will test your patience  given the game's world dense as it is  with intrusive outcrops and heavily  ornamented buildings but Treecko is an  impressive animal to behold with the  mannerisms of a house cat as he rests or  navigates complex environments and the  temperament of a lion during occasional  conflicts with magically powered  sentinels he swats and sniffs  curiosity's sometimes as a hint and  other times because he's simply  distracted when he's enraged the only  way to calm him is through the solace of  petting and coos when you look into  treecko's eyes as you slink out of reach  to pull a lever or when he senses  something frightening you don't see the  artifice that defines most video game  characters you see an honest portrayal  as cliche as it sounds Treecko feels  like a living and breathing animal and  you feel real concerned when you test  the limits of the environment under his  heft he is a brave beast that pulls off  unthinkable stunts feats that are  equally impressive and harrowing for  their inherent danger you often come  along for the ride  clinging to his plumage as he leaps high  above the ground obscured by billowing  fog his appearance is nuanced enriched  with hair and feathers that move with  the wind and jostle as you climb around  his body The Last Guardian is for the  most part a totally convincing  experience that draws you into the  mindset of Treecko and the boy however  there are times when you're reminded  that they do in fact inhabit a game from  beginning to end without an option to  disable it the HUD shows you button  prompts when you're in front of an  interactive object and despite handling  numerous impressive scenes without a  hitch there are rare events with obvious  framerate issues these occurrences by no  means dominate the game far from it but  they make you consider the technological  lattice holding the world together when  your intention should be focused on the  events at hand but beyond these moments  the last Guardians numerous delays and  shift a PlayStation 4 have obviously  paid off your journey is dominated by  awe-inspiring architecture and natural  wonder as you weave in and out of caves  and ruins you're treated to wide views  of towers and bridges that you may never  visit but they live on in your  imagination as you piece together the  story and the world around you  when you reach the end your affection  for Treecko and sympathy for both  characters blossoms The Last Guardian is  a powerful and moving experience for the  ways it makes you care about its  characters masterfully executed  personalities and expressions the  creature is the undeniable star of the  show exhibiting believable physicality  and emotional range but the boy is a  valuable example of how to be patient  and resilient when faced with unforeseen  challenges when the book closes on their  story it's not hard to open it again and  begin anew the bond you worked so hard  to forge remains and after it's all said  and done the emotions Treecko elicits  make you want to give him another chance  to be the patient and effective partner  he truly deserves  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

